{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>On the morrow of the Sabbath the priest shall wave it.</b> The intent is not the Sabbath of Creation but the morrow of the first festival day of Passover, and so Onkelos translated: מבתר יומא טבא — 'after the good day.' This is the fifteenth of Nisan, and until the sixteenth of Nisan arrives the Torah forbade eating of the new grain, and this is: ״ולחם וקלי וכרמל לא תאכלו וגו׳ חקת עולם לדרותיכם בכל מושבותיכם״. Here, regarding the prohibition of the new grain, it mentioned ״בכל מושבותיכם״ — 'in all your dwellings,' ״חקת עולם לדורותיכם״ — 'an everlasting statute for your generations,' to teach you that the new grain is forbidden by Torah law in every place, even outside the Land during our exile. Therefore it says ״בכל מושבותיכם״, for it is a commandment that applies to all generations in all dwellings, both in the Land and outside the Land. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: Resh Lakish said, three things depend on the Land and apply both in the Land and outside the Land — the new grain, orlah (uncircumcised fruit), and kilayim (diverse kinds): the new grain by Torah law, orlah as a halachah given to Moses at Sinai, and kilayim by decree of the scribes."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:46.443321352Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}